{"annotationScore": 5.0, "comments": [{"commentType": "FUNCTION", "molecule": "Ubiquitin", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P62979", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Ubiquitin exists either covalently attached to another protein, or free (unanchored). When covalently bound, it is conjugated to target proteins via an isopeptide bond either as a monomer (monoubiquitin), a polymer linked via different Lys residues of the ubiquitin (polyubiquitin chains) or a linear polymer linked via the initiator Met of the ubiquitin (linear polyubiquitin chains). Polyubiquitin chains, when attached to a target protein, have different functions depending on the Lys residue of the ubiquitin that is linked: Lys-48-linked is involved in protein degradation via the proteasome. Linear polymer chains formed via attachment by the initiator Met lead to cell signaling. Ubiquitin is usually conjugated to Lys residues of target proteins, however, in rare cases, conjugation to Cys or Ser residues has been observed. When polyubiquitin is free (unanchored-polyubiquitin), it also has distinct roles, such as in activation of protein kinases, and in signaling"}]}, {"commentType": "FUNCTION", "molecule": "Small ribosomal subunit protein eS31", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P62979", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Component of the 40S subunit of the ribosome. Part of the small subunit (SSU) processome, first precursor of the small eukaryotic ribosomal subunit. During the assembly of the SSU processome in the nucleolus, many ribosome biogenesis factors, an RNA chaperone and ribosomal proteins associate with the nascent pre-rRNA and work in concert to generate RNA folding, modifications, rearrangements and cleavage as well as targeted degradation of pre-ribosomal RNA by the RNA exosome"}]}, {"commentType": "SUBUNIT", "molecule": "Small ribosomal subunit protein eS31", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P62979", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Part of the 40S ribosomal subunit. Part of the small subunit (SSU) processome, composed of more than 70 proteins and the RNA chaperone small nucleolar RNA (snoRNA) U3"}]}, {"commentType": "SUBCELLULAR LOCATION", "molecule": "Ubiquitin", "subcellularLocations": [{"location": {"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250"}], "id": "SL-0086", "value": "Cytoplasm"}}, {"location": {"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250"}], "id": "SL-0191", "value": "Nucleus"}}]}, {"commentType": "SUBCELLULAR LOCATION", "molecule": "Small ribosomal subunit protein eS31", "subcellularLocations": [{"location": {"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P62979", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "id": "SL-0188", "value": "Nucleus, nucleolus"}}]}, {"commentType": "MISCELLANEOUS", "texts": [{"value": "In Drosophila ubiquitin is encoded by 3 different genes. RpL40 and RpS27A genes code for a single copy of ubiquitin fused to the ribosomal proteins eL40 and eS31, respectively. Ubi-p63E gene codes for a polyubiquitin precursor with exact head to tail repeats"}]}, {"commentType": "MISCELLANEOUS", "texts": [{"value": "For a better understanding, features related to ubiquitin are only indicated for the first chain"}]}, {"commentType": "SIMILARITY", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"}], "value": "In the N-terminal section; belongs to the ubiquitin family"}]}, {"commentType": "SIMILARITY", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"}], "value": "In the C-terminal section; belongs to the eukaryotic ribosomal protein eS31 family"}]}, {"commentType": "SEQUENCE CAUTION", "evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"}], "sequence": "CAA48871.1", "sequenceCautionType": "Erroneous gene model prediction"}], "entryAudit": {"entryVersion": 191, "firstPublicDate": "1990-04-01", "lastAnnotationUpdateDate": "2026-11-04", "lastSequenceUpdateDate": "2010-08-10", "sequenceVersion": 2}, "entryType": "UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)", "extraA